---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import HeroLabExplorer from "../components/HeroLabExplorer.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const cv = base + "David_Eti_Systems_Infrastructure_CV.pdf";
---
<BaseLayout title="Home" description="Explore the enterprise infrastructure David Eti built, validated and deliberately tested through real troubleshooting investigations.">
  <section class="exp-hero" aria-labelledby="exp-headline">
    <div class="container exp-hero-grid">
      <div class="exp-copy">
        <div class="exp-eyebrow">Nova Corp / Enterprise infrastructure lab</div>
        <h1 id="exp-headline">I built the infrastructure. <em>Then I tested what happens when it fails.</em></h1>
        <p class="exp-subtitle">A small fictional company. Real servers, network boundaries, identity systems and recovery tests from my home lab. Explore the architecture, then follow the evidence.</p>
        <div class="button-row">
          <a class="btn" href={base + "company/"}>Explore the infrastructure <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          <a class="btn ghost" href={base + "incidents/dns-client-outage/"}>Replay a failure <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        </div>
        <p class="exp-role"><strong>Built and tested by David Eti</strong><br/>Seeking system administrator, infrastructure and DevOps opportunities in Belgium and the Netherlands.</p>
        <div class="exp-contact" aria-label="Professional links">
          <a href={cv}>Download CV ↗</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/EtiDavid/Nova-Corp">GitHub ↗</a>
          <a href="mailto:etidavid26@gmail.com">Email ↗</a>
          <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/eti-david">LinkedIn ↗</a>
        </div>
        <p class="exp-honesty">Nova Corp is fictional; the infrastructure and recorded tests are real home-lab work. This is a dated architectural snapshot, not a live monitoring console.</p>
        <div class="exp-proofband" aria-label="Technologies demonstrated">
          <span>Windows Server + Active Directory</span>
          <span>Proxmox + pfSense</span>
          <span>PowerShell automation</span>
          <span>Verified restore tests</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <HeroLabExplorer />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="exp-block" aria-labelledby="exp-outcomes">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="exp-section-heading">
        <div class="exp-eyebrow">01 / Documented outcomes</div>
        <h2 id="exp-outcomes">I don't just configure systems. I test their failure modes.</h2>
        <p>Every example below is tied to an actual investigation, observed behavior and the scope of what the test proves.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="exp-stories">
        <article class="exp-story">
          <small>IDENTITY / CONTROLLED OUTAGE</small>
          <h3>One Domain Controller went offline.</h3>
          <p>DC01 was deliberately taken down to test whether the other Domain Controller could continue serving the environment.</p>
          <p class="result">DC02 handled the documented checks. Replication was checked after DC01 returned.</p>
          <a href={base + "incidents/dc01-outage/"}>Inspect the evidence ↗</a>
        </article>
        <article class="exp-story">
          <small>RECOVERY / FILE-LEVEL RESTORE</small>
          <h3>A file was deleted. Then restored, byte for byte.</h3>
          <p>A deliberately missing backup destination caused a failure. After repair, a disposable test file was restored.</p>
          <p class="result">Original and restored SHA-256 fingerprints matched.</p>
          <a href={base + "incidents/backup-target-unavailable/"}>Inspect the recovery ↗</a>
        </article>
        <article class="exp-story">
          <small>SECURITY / MANAGEMENT ACCESS</small>
          <h3>An employee network could reach firewall administration.</h3>
          <p>The firewall management path was accessible when it should not have been. Specific block rules were added ahead of the broad pass.</p>
          <p class="result">Management was blocked while required business access continued.</p>
          <a href={base + "incidents/firewall-management-exposure/"}>Inspect the investigation ↗</a>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="exp-block" aria-labelledby="exp-investigation">
    <div class="container exp-case">
      <div>
        <div class="exp-eyebrow">02 / Investigate the failure</div>
        <h2 id="exp-investigation">“The IT folder won't open.”</h2>
        <p>A shared folder failed by name. It would have been easy to blame the file server. Instead, I separated DNS resolution from basic network reachability to find out what was actually broken.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:17px">Follow five recorded diagnostic steps. Reveal each result, see what it proves and watch the relevant path change on the diagram.</p>
        <a href={base + "incidents/dns-client-outage/"} class="btn">Start the five-step investigation <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      </div>
      <div class="exp-case-visual" aria-label="A real recorded diagnostic command and outcome">
        <div class="exp-case-top"><span>INCIDENT REPLAY / DNS CLIENT</span><span>RECORDED TEST · NOV 2026</span></div>
        <div class="exp-case-content">
          <span class="exp-flag">SYMPTOM · SHARE FAILED BY NAME</span>
          <div class="exp-cmd"><span style="color:#7896B0">PS&gt;</span> Test-NetConnection 10.10.20.20 -Port 445<br/><br/><b>TcpTestSucceeded: True</b></div>
          <div class="exp-case-note">
            <span aria-hidden="true">↳</span>
            <span><b>What it established:</b> The file server answered on SMB port 445. The next checks focused on the workstation's name resolution rather than rebuilding the server.</span>
          </div>
          <p style="font-size:.74rem;color:#97ADC2;margin-top:15px">Recorded journal summary, not a live command or fabricated terminal screenshot.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="exp-block" aria-labelledby="exp-next">
    <div class="container exp-last">
      <div>
        <div class="exp-eyebrow">03 / Go deeper</div>
        <h2 id="exp-next">See the systems. Follow the decisions. Verify the work.</h2>
        <p class="lead">Start with the clickable infrastructure. Then explore the failure investigations, the change requests and the underlying engineering records. The details stay available without dominating the first impression.</p>
        <div class="button-row" style="margin-top:22px">
          <a class="btn" href={base + "tour/"}>Take the guided tour ↗</a>
          <a class="btn ghost" href={base + "sources/"}>View engineering evidence ↗</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <aside class="planned">
        <div class="exp-eyebrow">Next security milestone</div>
        <h3 style="margin-top:12px">CR-016 / Privileged administration</h3>
        <p>Separate administrative VPN access and stronger management-plane isolation. This is planned work, not a completed control.</p>
        <a href={base + "roadmap/"}>View completed and planned work ↗</a>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
